<h1>SDK Parity</h1>

<p>Three official SDKs are available: Python, Node.js, and Swift. This page tracks the API surface parity between them and the roadmap to close any gaps.</p>

<h2>Status at a glance</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Node.js: The package is `pilotprotocol` on npm. It is feature complete and includes TypeScript types, `using` support, and `Buffer` I/O. It is a reference surface alongside Python.</li>
  <li>Python: The package is `pilotprotocol` on PyPI. It is feature complete and includes full type hints (`py.typed`), context managers, and snake_case naming.</li>
  <li>Swift: The package is `sdk-swift` via SwiftPM. It provides core trust and datagrams, and uses an embedded daemon (XCFramework). Streams, networks, managed networks, policy, member tags, high-level services, and most registry admin features are not yet exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Summary by feature category</h2>
<p>The summary is based on an audit from 2026-05-28 against `sdk-node@d02bd00`, `sdk-python@93584ea`, and `sdk-swift@0d49f87`.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Lifecycle (construct, dispose): Supported in Node, Python, and Swift. Each SDK uses its native idiom (`using`, `with`, `deinit`).</li>
  <li>Daemon admin — `info`, `health`: Supported in Node, Python, and Swift.</li>
  <li>Daemon admin — `rotateKey`: Supported in Node and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Trust — initiate handshake, list trusted peers: Supported in Node, Python, and Swift.</li>
  <li>Trust admin — approve/reject/pending/revoke: Supported in Node and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Trust convenience — `waitForTrust`: Supported in Swift only. Planned for Node and Python.</li>
  <li>Datagrams — `sendTo`, `recvFrom`: Supported in Node, Python, and Swift. Swift uses `send(to:port:data:)` and returns a typed `Datagram`.</li>
  <li>Datagrams — `broadcast`: Supported in Node and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Streams — `dial`, `listen`, `Conn`, `Listener`: Supported in Node and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Registry admin — hostname / visibility / deregister / tags / webhook: Supported in Node and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Networks — list/join/leave/members/invite/respond: Supported in Node and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Managed networks — score/status/rankings/cycle/reconcile: Supported in Node and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Policy — get/set: Supported in Node and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Member tags — get/set: Supported in Node and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>High-level services — `sendMessage`, `sendFile`, `publishEvent`, `subscribeEvent`: Supported in Node and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>FFI loader — `findLibrary` / `loadLibrary`: Supported in Node, private in Python, and not applicable to Swift. Swift embeds the library in an XCFramework.</li>
  <li>Typed response structs (`Config`, `StartResult`, `Datagram`, `Error`): Supported in Swift only. This is a Swift idiom; Node and Python return untyped objects.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What counts as a gap</h2>
<p>Naming differences across languages are not considered gaps. The matrix collapses idiomatic equivalents into a single canonical row.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Constructors: `new Driver()` (Node), `Driver()` (Python), and `Pilot.start(config)` (Swift) are equivalent.</li>
  <li>Cleanup: `driver.close()`, `pilot.stop()`, Python's `with` statement, and Node's `using` statement are equivalent.</li>
  <li>Naming convention: Python's `send_message` maps to Node/Swift's `sendMessage`.</li>
  <li>Datagram receive: Node and Python return a dictionary, while Swift returns a typed `Datagram` struct. Both surface the same underlying RPC.</li>
</ul>
<p>A real gap is an operation that one SDK does not expose.</p>

<h2>Full method matrix</h2>
<p>The complete row-per-method matrix is generated by a deterministic script in the main protocol repo (`scripts/parity-audit/`). The script produces an updated `matrix.csv` file.</p>

<h2>Roadmap</h2>
<p>The end-state target is full parity across all three SDKs, except for intentional differences like the FFI loader and Swift's typed response structs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Swift: Work is tracked to add streams, networks, managed networks, policy, member tags, registry admin, trust admin, and high-level services.</li>
  <li>Node and Python: Add `waitForTrust(peerId, timeoutMs)`.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cross-SDK versioning is documented in the GOVERNANCE file in the main protocol repo. All three SDKs share the same MAJOR.MINOR version line and have coordinated releases when the wire protocol changes.</p>

